THIRD-PARTY WORK, AND HOW IT IS USED

The subject of this exercise is Kronecker Embeddings, arXiv:2605.29459v1. Its
reference implementation is https://github.com/theschoolofai/kronecker-embeddings,
which is Apache-2.0 licensed. The codec here is a reimplementation written
against the paper's Equation 1 and that repository read as a specification; it
is not a vendored copy, and no file from it is redistributed.

One difference is worth recording rather than leaving to be discovered. The
reference codec indexes the grid as `byte_value * d_p + position`; this
implementation uses `position * 256 + byte_value`. The two are a permutation of
each other's coordinates, and since the projection W is learned, they are
equivalent for training and for recovery -- but a direct numerical diff against
the reference will not line up without accounting for it.

The frozen 10,000-token tokenizer used for every vocabulary measurement is this
repository's own, produced in exercise 02 and loaded through exercise 04's
`datacleaning.tokens`. Collision counts, byte-length distributions and recovery
rates are properties of THAT vocabulary and do not transfer unchanged to another.

WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT

Every training figure published by this exercise is measured on one Apple
Silicon laptop: a 2-layer transformer, d_model 256, 500 steps, batch 8 x 64,
over roughly 200,000 tokens of real text. The claims are ABOUT models three to
four orders of magnitude larger. They were never run at that scale. Nothing here
establishes that a result holds at 124M parameters or after 100,000 steps, and
weight tying in particular is known to behave differently late in training.

Recovery figures are measured on 2,000 tokens sampled from the frozen
vocabulary, with both random and trained projection matrices, and the trained
case is stated separately because it is the one that could have failed.

WHAT IS A NEGATIVE RESULT, AND SAID SO

Three claims made during this work turned out to be false and are corrected
where they were made rather than quietly removed: that a d x d transform on the
hidden state gives the tied head expressive freedom (it does not -- it
reparameterises h); that folding byte positions "loses nothing recoverable" (it
does -- folding records a multiset, not a sequence); and an earlier recovery
figure that came from reading a metric which scores truncated tokens as decoder
failures by construction. The Fourier position variant is also a negative
result: it removes every collision and trains worse than doing nothing.

WHICH PROBLEM EACH RESULT ANSWERS

The requirements states that its five problems are separate. Every result here is
labelled with the problem it addresses, and any result that spans two is
labelled as spanning two rather than being presented as a single-problem
solution. See README.md.

